# Visual review

Run this on anything that the audience sees rendered: a screen, page, component, email, slide, chart, or generated image. Skip it for logic-only changes.

## Capture

- Render the real result. Take a screenshot with the browser or screenshot tool that the session has.
- When the layout is responsive, capture a wide view and a phone-width view.
- Capture the states that change the layout: empty, loading, long content, error, success, and focus.
- If you cannot render it, finish the work. Then say that it was not reviewed visually, and ask the user for a screenshot. Do not claim a visual review that you did not do.

## Analyze the image

Describe what is in the image before you judge it. Judge the pixels, not what the code meant to do.

1. First glance. What does the eye land on first? Is it the one thing that the audience should do?
2. Reading order. Name what is read first, second, and third.
3. Squint. Blur the image or view it in grayscale. The hierarchy should hold without color.
4. Breakage. Clipped or overflowing text, overlap, bad wraps, broken images, horizontal scroll, layout that shifts on hover, loading, or long content.
5. Grouping. Related things sit closer together than unrelated things.
6. Consistency. The same element has the same size, color, and spacing everywhere.

## Rules of thumb

- One primary action per view, and it is the most prominent thing there.
- Spacing comes from one scale. Space between groups is larger than space inside a group.
- At most two type families and a few sizes. Body text around 16px, lines around 45 to 75 characters.
- One accent color. Body text contrast at least 4.5:1. Large text, control edges, and focus rings at least 3:1. Color is never the only signal.
- Focus is visible on every control.
- Targets at least 24px everywhere, and about 44px on touch screens.
- Motion answers an action or marks one moment. No entrance animation on every section. Respect reduced motion.
- Real content, not placeholder text. Each label is the shortest one that tells the next action.
- Every element aligns with something. Use a few shared edges.
- If the audience would not miss a section, remove it.

## Loop

Name each issue by where it is in the image and the fix. Fix, capture again, and compare. Stop after three rounds and report what is left.

```
Bad: Looks good. (Judged from reading the code.)
Good: Phone width: the primary button wraps under the heading and falls below the fold. Moved it above the details. The second capture shows it in the first view.
```
